## 1. Identify the branches

State the current branch, and **ask which branch to target**. Never assume.

<!-- DECIDE: what is the default target, and what does targeting anything else mean?

     Most projects have one integration branch that features merge into, and a separate branch that
     is deployable. If a merge to one of them ships to real users, say so *here* — that is the single
     most useful sentence in this document, and its absence is how someone deploys by accident. -->

## 2. Uncommitted changes

Run `git status`. If there is uncommitted work, say what it is and **ask before committing it**.
Never stash, discard, or commit silently.

This rule exists because the alternative loses work that was never yours to judge — and because an
unrelated file swept into a commit by a blanket `git add` is discovered much later, in someone else's
PR.

## 3. Update the target, then rebase

```bash
git fetch origin
git checkout <target> && git pull --ff-only origin <target>
git checkout <current>
git rebase <target>
```

If `--ff-only` fails the target has diverged locally — stop and flag it rather than forcing it.

<!-- DECIDE: rebase or merge? Rebase keeps history linear, which makes `git blame` and
     `git log --follow` meaningful — worth stating the reason, because whoever hits their first
     painful conflict will otherwise ask why. -->

## 4. Conflicts

Resolve none of them autonomously. For each: summarise in plain language what each side does and what
losing it would mean, then **ask an open question** — not "ours or theirs", since the answer is often
a combination or a rewrite. Keep asking until the resolution is unambiguous.

A wrong conflict resolution is harder to undo than an extra question.

## 5. Push

```bash
git push --force-with-lease origin <current>
```

`--force-with-lease` rather than `--force`: it aborts if someone else pushed since your last fetch.
On a shared branch that difference is the difference between a rebase and losing a colleague's work.

## 6. PR description template

```markdown
## Summary
- <what changed and why>

## Changes
- <bullet per meaningful change>

## Test plan
- [ ] <how this was verified>

## Verification tier

Verification-Tier: <unverified | self-tested | cross-verified | human-reviewed>
Verified-By: <name, role, or tool>
```

<!-- DECIDE: extend the test plan with this project's actual verification steps — which platforms
     get tested, which checks must pass. Keep them as checkboxes: `/draft-pr-description` copies this
     block verbatim, so whatever is here is what every PR is held to. Vague items produce vague PRs. -->

The `Verification-Tier` trailer mirrors the `Co-Authored-By:` commit trailer convention —
machine-parseable, diffable, and part of PR history rather than a GitHub label. Vocabulary and the
"lowest tier wins across a PR's commits" rule come from
`plugins/design-suite/templates/verification-tier.schema.json` (issue #55). **An agent drafting this
PR may self-report `unverified` or `self-tested`; it must never self-assign `cross-verified` or
`human-reviewed`** — those require an actual independent party, not the author's own say-so.
`/draft-pr-description` should leave this line for the human/agent opening the PR to fill in, not
guess a tier on their behalf.

